<section id="S3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S3.SS2.4" class="ltx_text">III-B</span> </span><span id="S3.SS2.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Adversarial Autoencoder Training</span></h3>

<div id="S3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Since we want the autoencoder to generate mel-spectrograms that will eventually be transformed into realistic audio waveforms, we train it using both a regular reconstruction loss and an adversarial loss that evaluates the signals generated by a pretrained, fixed vocoder. Specifically, we train the autoencoder alongside a waveform discriminator, so the autoencoder improves at generating mel-spectrograms from which a pretrained vocoder can produce realistic waveforms, while the discriminator improves at distinguishing those generated waveforms as fake data.
Additionally, since the DPM assumes the data samples to be zero-centered and have unit variance, we want the autoencoder bottleneck to satisfy this condition as well. To achieve this, we include a Kullback-Leibler (KL) divergence between the distribution of the bottleneck features and a standard normal distribution in the training loss, similar to VAE training.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">In the following, we use <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\mathbf{y}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐲</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\mathbf{y}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\mathbf{x}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐱</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\mathbf{x}}</annotation></semantics></math> to denote the waveform and its mel-spectrogram of a training speech sample, respectively.
Using <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\mathbf{g}}_{\phi}(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐠</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\mathbf{g}}_{\phi}(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\mathbf{f}}_{\phi}(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐟</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\mathbf{f}}_{\phi}(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> to denote the encoder and decoder of the autoencoder with parameter <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϕ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi</annotation></semantics></math>, the regular reconstruction loss <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\rm rec}(\phi)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>rec</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ϕ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\rm rec}(\phi)</annotation></semantics></math> is defined as an L1 loss in the mel-spectrogram domain:</p>
<table id="S3.EGx6" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E7"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathcal{J}_{\rm rec}(\phi)=\mathbb{E}_{\bm{\mathbf{x}}}[\|\bm{\mathbf{g}}_{\phi}(\bm{\mathbf{f}}_{\phi}(\bm{\mathbf{x}}))-\bm{\mathbf{x}}\|_{1}]." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒥</mi><mi>rec</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ϕ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mi>𝐱</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝐠</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐟</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>𝐱</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathcal{J}_{\rm rec}(\phi)=\mathbb{E}_{\bm{\mathbf{x}}}[\|\bm{\mathbf{g}}_{\phi}(\bm{\mathbf{f}}_{\phi}(\bm{\mathbf{x}}))-\bm{\mathbf{x}}\|_{1}].</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(7)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.p2.2" class="ltx_p">For the waveform discriminator, we use a combination of the multi-period discriminator (MPD) and multi-scale discriminator (MSD) introduced in HiFi-GAN <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib54" title="" class="ltx_ref">54</a>]</cite> and MelGAN <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib55" title="" class="ltx_ref">55</a>]</cite>.
Using <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{\psi}(\cdot)\in[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>ψ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{\psi}(\cdot)\in[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math> to denote the MPD and MSD treated as a single discriminator with parameter <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ψ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi</annotation></semantics></math>, the adversarial losses for the autoencoder and discriminator, <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\rm adv}^{\rm(a)}(\phi)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>adv</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ϕ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\rm adv}^{\rm(a)}(\phi)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\rm adv}^{\rm(d)}(\psi)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>adv</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ψ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\rm adv}^{\rm(d)}(\psi)</annotation></semantics></math>, are defined as the least squares GAN objectives <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib56" title="" class="ltx_ref">56</a>]</cite></p>
<table id="S3.EGx7" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E8"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathcal{J}_{\rm adv}^{\rm(a)}(\phi)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒥</mi><mi>adv</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ϕ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathcal{J}_{\rm adv}^{\rm(a)}(\phi)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\mathbb{E}_{\bm{\mathbf{x}}}[(d_{\psi}(\hat{\bm{\mathbf{y}}})-1)^{2}]," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mi>𝐱</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>ψ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\mathbb{E}_{\bm{\mathbf{x}}}[(d_{\psi}(\hat{\bm{\mathbf{y}}})-1)^{2}],</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(8)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S3.E9"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathcal{J}_{\rm adv}^{\rm(d)}(\psi)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒥</mi><mi>adv</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ψ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathcal{J}_{\rm adv}^{\rm(d)}(\psi)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\mathbb{E}_{\bm{\mathbf{y}}}[(d_{\psi}(\bm{\mathbf{y}})-1)^{2}]+\mathbb{E}_{\bm{\mathbf{x}}}[(d_{\psi}(\hat{\bm{\mathbf{y}}}))^{2}]," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mi>𝐲</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>ψ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐲</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mi>𝐱</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>ψ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\mathbb{E}_{\bm{\mathbf{y}}}[(d_{\psi}(\bm{\mathbf{y}})-1)^{2}]+\mathbb{E}_{\bm{\mathbf{x}}}[(d_{\psi}(\hat{\bm{\mathbf{y}}}))^{2}],</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(9)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.p2.3" class="ltx_p">where
<math id="S3.SS2.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\mathbf{h}}(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐡</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\mathbf{h}}(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> represents a pretrained (frozen) neural vocoder that transforms a mel-spectrogram into a waveform and
<math id="S3.SS2.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\bm{\mathbf{y}}}=\bm{\mathbf{h}}(\bm{\mathbf{g}}_{\phi}(\bm{\mathbf{f}}_{\phi}(\bm{\mathbf{x}}))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>𝐡</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐠</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐟</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em">CLOSE</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\bm{\mathbf{y}}}=\bm{\mathbf{h}}(\bm{\mathbf{g}}_{\phi}(\bm{\mathbf{f}}_{\phi}(\bm{\mathbf{x}}))</annotation></semantics></math> is a waveform generated by the neural vocoder from the autoencoder output.
In this paper, we used the pretrained universal HiFi-GAN vocoder taken from the official HiFi-GAN repository<span id="footnote1" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup>
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">1</span>
              
              
            https://github.com/jik876/hifi-gan</span></span></span>
for <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\mathbf{h}}(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐡</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\mathbf{h}}(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math>.
Minimizing Eq. (<a href="#S3.E9" title="In III-B Adversarial Autoencoder Training ‣ III Proposed: LatentVoiceGrad ‣ LatentVoiceGrad: Nonparallel Voice Conversion with Latent Diffusion/Flow-Matching Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a>) with respect to <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ψ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi</annotation></semantics></math>
means training the discriminator to classify real speech samples as 1 and the samples synthesized by the generator (i.e., autoencoder and vocoder) as 0.
Conversely, minimizing Eq. (<a href="#S3.E8" title="In III-B Adversarial Autoencoder Training ‣ III Proposed: LatentVoiceGrad ‣ LatentVoiceGrad: Nonparallel Voice Conversion with Latent Diffusion/Flow-Matching Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a>) with respect to <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϕ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi</annotation></semantics></math> means training the autoencoder to deceive the discriminator, i.e., to make the discriminator classify the synthesized samples as 1.
As in the HiFi-GAN training, we further incorporate the mel-spectrogram and feature matching losses</p>
<table id="S3.EGx8" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E10"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathcal{J}_{\rm mel}(\phi)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒥</mi><mi>mel</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ϕ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathcal{J}_{\rm mel}(\phi)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E10.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\mathbb{E}_{\bm{\mathbf{x}}}[\|\bm{\mathbf{m}}(\hat{\bm{\mathbf{y}}})-\bm{\mathbf{x}}\|_{1}]," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mi>𝐱</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>𝐦</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>𝐱</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\mathbb{E}_{\bm{\mathbf{x}}}[\|\bm{\mathbf{m}}(\hat{\bm{\mathbf{y}}})-\bm{\mathbf{x}}\|_{1}],</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(10)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S3.E11"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathcal{J}_{\rm feat}(\phi)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒥</mi><mi>feat</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ϕ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathcal{J}_{\rm feat}(\phi)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E11.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\mathbb{E}_{(\bm{\mathbf{x}},\bm{\mathbf{y}})}\left[\sum_{i=1}^{T}\frac{1}{N_{i}}\|d_{\psi}^{(i)}(\hat{\bm{\mathbf{y}}})-d_{\psi}^{(i)}(\bm{\mathbf{y}})\|_{1}\right]," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐲</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>T</mi></munderover></mstyle><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>d</mi><mi>ψ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>d</mi><mi>ψ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐲</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\mathbb{E}_{(\bm{\mathbf{x}},\bm{\mathbf{y}})}\left[\sum_{i=1}^{T}\frac{1}{N_{i}}\|d_{\psi}^{(i)}(\hat{\bm{\mathbf{y}}})-d_{\psi}^{(i)}(\bm{\mathbf{y}})\|_{1}\right],</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(11)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.p2.4" class="ltx_p">in the training objective for the autoencoder, where
<math id="S3.SS2.p2.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\mathbf{m}}(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐦</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\mathbf{m}}(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> represents the process of computing the mel-spectrogram from a waveform,
<math id="S3.SS2.p2.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> is the number of the layers in the discriminator,
and <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{\psi}^{(i)}(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>d</mi><mi>ψ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{\psi}^{(i)}(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the features and the number of features in the <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>-th layer of the discriminator, respectively.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The KL loss <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{J}_{\rm KL}(\phi)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒥</mi><mi>KL</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ϕ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{J}_{\rm KL}(\phi)</annotation></semantics></math> to regularize the latent <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\mathbf{z}}=\bm{\mathbf{g}}_{\phi}(\bm{\mathbf{x}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐳</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐠</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\mathbf{z}}=\bm{\mathbf{g}}_{\phi}(\bm{\mathbf{x}})</annotation></semantics></math> to be zero-centered and have unit variance is defined as</p>
<table id="S3.EGx9" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E12"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathcal{J}_{\rm KL}(\phi)=\frac{1}{2}\mathbb{E}_{\bm{\mathbf{x}}}[\sigma^{2}(\bm{\mathbf{x}})+\mu(\bm{\mathbf{x}})^{2}-1-\log\sigma^{2}(\bm{\mathbf{x}})]," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒥</mi><mi>KL</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ϕ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mi>𝐱</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msup><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathcal{J}_{\rm KL}(\phi)=\frac{1}{2}\mathbb{E}_{\bm{\mathbf{x}}}[\sigma^{2}(\bm{\mathbf{x}})+\mu(\bm{\mathbf{x}})^{2}-1-\log\sigma^{2}(\bm{\mathbf{x}})],</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(12)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.p3.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu(\bm{\mathbf{x}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu(\bm{\mathbf{x}})</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma^{2}(\bm{\mathbf{x}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma^{2}(\bm{\mathbf{x}})</annotation></semantics></math> denote the mean and variance of the elements of <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\mathbf{z}}=\bm{\mathbf{g}}_{\phi}(\bm{\mathbf{x}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐳</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐠</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\mathbf{z}}=\bm{\mathbf{g}}_{\phi}(\bm{\mathbf{x}})</annotation></semantics></math>, respectively.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Putting it all together, the training objectives to minimize with respect to <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϕ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ψ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi</annotation></semantics></math> are given as
<math id="S3.SS2.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{J}_{\rm ae}(\phi)=\lambda(\mathcal{J}_{\rm rec}(\phi)+\mathcal{J}_{\rm mel}(\phi)+\mathcal{J}_{\rm KL}(\phi))+\mathcal{J}_{\rm adv}^{\rm(a)}(\phi)+\mathcal{J}_{\rm feat}(\phi)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒥</mi><mi>ae</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ϕ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒥</mi><mi>rec</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ϕ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒥</mi><mi>mel</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ϕ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒥</mi><mi>KL</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ϕ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒥</mi><mi>adv</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ϕ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒥</mi><mi>feat</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ϕ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{J}_{\rm ae}(\phi)=\lambda(\mathcal{J}_{\rm rec}(\phi)+\mathcal{J}_{\rm mel}(\phi)+\mathcal{J}_{\rm KL}(\phi))+\mathcal{J}_{\rm adv}^{\rm(a)}(\phi)+\mathcal{J}_{\rm feat}(\phi)</annotation></semantics></math>
and
<math id="S3.SS2.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{J}_{\rm dis}(\psi)=\mathcal{J}_{\rm adv}^{\rm(d)}(\psi)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒥</mi><mi>dis</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ψ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒥</mi><mi>adv</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ψ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{J}_{\rm dis}(\psi)=\mathcal{J}_{\rm adv}^{\rm(d)}(\psi)</annotation></semantics></math>,
respectively, where <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math> is a regularization parameter that weighs the importance of <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{J}_{\rm rec}(\phi)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒥</mi><mi>rec</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ϕ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{J}_{\rm rec}(\phi)</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{J}_{\rm mel}(\phi)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒥</mi><mi>mel</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ϕ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{J}_{\rm mel}(\phi)</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{J}_{\rm KL}(\phi)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒥</mi><mi>KL</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ϕ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{J}_{\rm KL}(\phi)</annotation></semantics></math>.
In this paper, we set <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math> to 45.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p5.1" class="ltx_p">In the current experiment, the channel numbers of the autoencoder bottleneck and the mel-spectrogram were set to 32 and 80, respectively.
The architectures of the encoder and decoder were each implemented as a stack consisting of a fully connected layer (with 256 output channels) followed by a leaky rectified linear unit (LReLU) activation, two-layer bidirectional long short-term memory (BiLSTM) networks (with 256 output channels), and another fully connected layer (with 32 output channels for the encoder and 80 output channels for the decoder).</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S3.SS3.4" class="ltx_text">III-C</span> </span><span id="S3.SS3.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">DPM Training</span></h3>

<div id="S3.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">While VoiceGrad works by gradually transforming a source mel-spectrogram into a target mel-spectrogram through a reverse diffusion process, LatentVoiceGrad uses the autoencoder bottleneck features of the source mel-spectrogram as the data to be transformed (Fig. <a href="#S3.F1.fig1" title="Fig. 1 ‣ III-A Basic Idea ‣ III Proposed: LatentVoiceGrad ‣ LatentVoiceGrad: Nonparallel Voice Conversion with Latent Diffusion/Flow-Matching Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>).
Once the autoencoder has been trained as described above, the score network is trained on the autoencoder bottleneck features extracted from the training speech samples of various speakers. Like in VoiceGrad, the score network is conditioned on the speaker embedding <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\mathbf{s}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐬</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\mathbf{s}}</annotation></semantics></math>, timestep <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>l</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l</annotation></semantics></math>, and phoneme embedding sequence <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\mathbf{p}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐩</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\mathbf{p}}</annotation></semantics></math>.
If we use <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\mathbf{z}}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\mathbf{z}}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> to denote the autoencoder bottleneck features extracted from the mel-spectrogram <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\mathbf{x}}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\mathbf{x}}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> of a training utterance, the training objective remains the same as in Eq. (<a href="#S2.E6" title="In II-B Application to Voice Conversion ‣ II VoiceGrad ‣ LatentVoiceGrad: Nonparallel Voice Conversion with Latent Diffusion/Flow-Matching Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>), but with <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\mathbf{x}}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\mathbf{x}}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> replaced by <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\mathbf{z}}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\mathbf{z}}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>.
After the score network is trained, input speech can be converted to a target speaker’s voice by transforming its audio signal into the bottleneck features using the encoder <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\mathbf{f}}_{\phi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐟</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\mathbf{f}}_{\phi}</annotation></semantics></math>, modifying them through the reverse diffusion process using the target speaker embedding <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\mathbf{s}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐬</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\mathbf{s}}</annotation></semantics></math>, and finally generating a waveform using the decoder <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\mathbf{g}}_{\phi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐠</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\mathbf{g}}_{\phi}</annotation></semantics></math> and vocoder <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\mathbf{h}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐡</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\mathbf{h}}</annotation></semantics></math> (Algorithm <a href="#alg2" title="Algorithm 2 ‣ III-C DPM Training ‣ III Proposed: LatentVoiceGrad ‣ LatentVoiceGrad: Nonparallel Voice Conversion with Latent Diffusion/Flow-Matching Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<figure id="alg2" class="ltx_float ltx_float_algorithm ltx_framed ltx_framed_top">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_float"><span id="alg2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Algorithm 2</span> </span> LatentVoiceGrad (DPM version) VC process</figcaption>
<div id="alg2.3" class="ltx_listing ltx_framed ltx_framed_topbottom ltx_listing">
<div id="alg2.l0" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg2.l0.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">0:</span></span>
              
             <math id="alg2.l0.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\alpha_{l}\}_{l=1}^{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>L</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\alpha_{l}\}_{l=1}^{L}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="alg2.l0.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\bar{\alpha}_{l}\}_{l=1}^{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>α</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>l</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>L</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\bar{\alpha}_{l}\}_{l=1}^{L}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="alg2.l0.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\mathbf{x}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐱</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\mathbf{x}}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="alg2.l0.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\mathbf{x}}_{\rm ref}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>ref</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\mathbf{x}}_{\rm ref}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="alg2.l0.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>L</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg2.l1" class="ltx_listingline">
              
             <math id="alg2.l1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\mathbf{s}}\leftarrow\mathsf{SpeakerEncoder}(\bm{\mathbf{x}}_{\rm ref})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐬</mi><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><mi>𝖲𝗉𝖾𝖺𝗄𝖾𝗋𝖤𝗇𝖼𝗈𝖽𝖾𝗋</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>ref</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\mathbf{s}}\leftarrow\mathsf{SpeakerEncoder}(\bm{\mathbf{x}}_{\rm ref})</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg2.l2" class="ltx_listingline">
              
             <math id="alg2.l2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\mathbf{p}}\leftarrow\mathsf{PhonemeEncoder}(\bm{\mathbf{x}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐩</mi><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><mi>𝖯𝗁𝗈𝗇𝖾𝗆𝖾𝖤𝗇𝖼𝗈𝖽𝖾𝗋</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\mathbf{p}}\leftarrow\mathsf{PhonemeEncoder}(\bm{\mathbf{x}})</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg2.l3" class="ltx_listingline">
              
             <math id="alg2.l3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\mathbf{z}}\leftarrow\bm{\mathbf{f}}_{\phi}(\bm{\mathbf{x}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐳</mi><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐟</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\mathbf{z}}\leftarrow\bm{\mathbf{f}}_{\phi}(\bm{\mathbf{x}})</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg2.l4" class="ltx_listingline">
              
             <span id="alg2.l4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">for</span> <math id="alg2.l4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l=L^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>L</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l=L^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="alg2.l4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="alg2.l4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">do</span>



</div>
<div id="alg2.l5" class="ltx_listingline">
              
              Draw <math id="alg2.l5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\mathbf{\epsilon}}\sim\mathcal{N}(\bm{\mathbf{0}},\bm{\mathbf{I}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϵ</mi><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>𝟎</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐈</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\mathbf{\epsilon}}\sim\mathcal{N}(\bm{\mathbf{0}},\bm{\mathbf{I}})</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg2.l6" class="ltx_listingline">
              
              Update
<math id="alg2.l6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\mathbf{z}}\leftarrow\frac{1}{\sqrt{\alpha_{l}}}\left(\bm{\mathbf{z}}-\frac{1-\alpha_{l}}{\sqrt{1-\bar{\alpha}_{l}}}\bm{\mathbf{\epsilon}}_{\theta}(\bm{\mathbf{z}},l,\bm{\mathbf{s}},\bm{\mathbf{p}})\right)+\nu_{l}\bm{\mathbf{\epsilon}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐳</mi><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msqrt><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>l</mi></msub></msqrt></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mi>𝐳</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>l</mi></msub></mrow><msqrt><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>α</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>l</mi></msub></mrow></msqrt></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϵ</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐳</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>l</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐬</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐩</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ν</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϵ</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\mathbf{z}}\leftarrow\frac{1}{\sqrt{\alpha_{l}}}\left(\bm{\mathbf{z}}-\frac{1-\alpha_{l}}{\sqrt{1-\bar{\alpha}_{l}}}\bm{\mathbf{\epsilon}}_{\theta}(\bm{\mathbf{z}},l,\bm{\mathbf{s}},\bm{\mathbf{p}})\right)+\nu_{l}\bm{\mathbf{\epsilon}}</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg2.l7" class="ltx_listingline">
              
             <span id="alg2.l7.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">end</span> <span id="alg2.l7.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">for</span>
</div>
<div id="alg2.l8" class="ltx_listingline">
              
             <math id="alg2.l8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\mathbf{y}}\leftarrow\bm{\mathbf{h}}(\bm{\mathbf{g}}_{\phi}(\bm{\mathbf{z}}))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐲</mi><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><mi>𝐡</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐠</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐳</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\mathbf{y}}\leftarrow\bm{\mathbf{h}}(\bm{\mathbf{g}}_{\phi}(\bm{\mathbf{z}}))</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg2.l9" class="ltx_listingline">
              
             <span id="alg2.l9.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">return</span>  <math id="alg2.l9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\mathbf{y}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐲</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\mathbf{y}}</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
</div>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S3.SS4.4" class="ltx_text">III-D</span> </span><span id="S3.SS4.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Flow Matching Approach</span></h3>

<div id="S3.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">When the number of timesteps in DPMs approaches infinity, their diffusion process can be understood as a stochastic differential equation (SDE). In this view, sampling data via reverse diffusion corresponds to numerically solving the reverse-time SDE or the associated probability-flow ordinary differential equation (ODE).
Recently, a new class of generative models known as flow matching (FM) models <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib51" title="" class="ltx_ref">51</a>]</cite> has emerged as an advanced version of continuous normalizing flows (CNF) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib57" title="" class="ltx_ref">57</a>]</cite>,
and there has been a growing interest in exploring their application to VC tasks <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib58" title="" class="ltx_ref">58</a>, <a href="#bib.bib49" title="" class="ltx_ref">49</a>]</cite>.
Unlike DPMs, which learn the score function of an SDE, FM models aim to directly learn the vector field of an arbitrary ODE. They use a neural network to approximate this vector field, which can then be used to numerically solve the ODE to generate data samples. These models often design their ODEs and vector fields to form straight line sampling trajectories and minimize transport costs, resulting in simpler, more efficient formulations and better-quality outcomes.
Given this advantage, we investigate using an FM model as an alternative to the DPM to see if it can further accelerate the VoiceGrad conversion process without compromising speech quality.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Here, we review the origins and rationale behind the FM concept.
Let <math id="S3.SS4.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\mathbf{x}}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\mathbf{x}}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> represent a data sample generated according to an unknown data distribution <math id="S3.SS4.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q_{1}(\bm{\mathbf{x}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q_{1}(\bm{\mathbf{x}})</annotation></semantics></math>.
FM models, like CNF models, assume that <math id="S3.SS4.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\mathbf{x}}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\mathbf{x}}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> is obtained by gradually transforming an initial point <math id="S3.SS4.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\mathbf{x}}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\mathbf{x}}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> in the data space through an ODE:</p>
<table id="S3.EGx10" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E13"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\frac{\mbox{d}\bm{\mathbf{x}}_{t}}{\mbox{d}t}=\bm{\mathbf{u}}_{t}(\bm{\mathbf{x}}_{t})," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mtext>d</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mrow><mtext>d</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐮</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\frac{\mbox{d}\bm{\mathbf{x}}_{t}}{\mbox{d}t}=\bm{\mathbf{u}}_{t}(\bm{\mathbf{x}}_{t}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(13)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS4.p2.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS4.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> represents time starting from 0 and ending at 1,
<math id="S3.SS4.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\mathbf{x}}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\mathbf{x}}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> represents the value at time <math id="S3.SS4.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>,
and
<math id="S3.SS4.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\mathbf{u}}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐮</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\mathbf{u}}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> represents the vector field governing the dynamics of the ODE.
Note that in the description of DPM in Subsection <a href="#S2.SS1" title="II-A Diffusion probabilistic models ‣ II VoiceGrad ‣ LatentVoiceGrad: Nonparallel Voice Conversion with Latent Diffusion/Flow-Matching Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">II-A</span></a>, the data sample was denoted as <math id="S3.SS4.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\mathbf{x}}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\mathbf{x}}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>, whereas here it is denoted as <math id="S3.SS4.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\mathbf{x}}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\mathbf{x}}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>, following the literature on FM models, with the initial point of the ODE denoted as <math id="S3.SS4.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\mathbf{x}}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\mathbf{x}}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>.
The core concept in CNF and FM models, which builds on neural ODEs <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib57" title="" class="ltx_ref">57</a>]</cite>, is to represent <math id="S3.SS4.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\mathbf{u}}_{t}(\bm{\mathbf{x}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐮</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\mathbf{u}}_{t}(\bm{\mathbf{x}})</annotation></semantics></math> using a neural network <math id="S3.SS4.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\mathbf{v}}_{\theta}(\bm{\mathbf{x}},t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐯</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\mathbf{v}}_{\theta}(\bm{\mathbf{x}},t)</annotation></semantics></math> with parameter <math id="S3.SS4.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta</annotation></semantics></math>.
If the uniqueness of the solution of Eq. (<a href="#S3.E13" title="In III-D Flow Matching Approach ‣ III Proposed: LatentVoiceGrad ‣ LatentVoiceGrad: Nonparallel Voice Conversion with Latent Diffusion/Flow-Matching Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">13</span></a>) is satisfied,
Eq. (<a href="#S3.E13" title="In III-D Flow Matching Approach ‣ III Proposed: LatentVoiceGrad ‣ LatentVoiceGrad: Nonparallel Voice Conversion with Latent Diffusion/Flow-Matching Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">13</span></a>) defines a diffeomorphism
<math id="S3.SS4.p2.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\mathbf{x}}_{t}=\bm{\mathbf{\phi}}_{t}(\bm{\mathbf{x}}_{0})=\bm{\mathbf{x}}_{0}+\int_{0}^{t}\bm{\mathbf{u}}_{s}(\bm{\mathbf{x}}_{s})\mbox{d}s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϕ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo rspace="0.055em">+</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∫</mo><mn>0</mn><mi>t</mi></msubsup><mrow><msub><mi>𝐮</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>d</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\mathbf{x}}_{t}=\bm{\mathbf{\phi}}_{t}(\bm{\mathbf{x}}_{0})=\bm{\mathbf{x}}_{0}+\int_{0}^{t}\bm{\mathbf{u}}_{s}(\bm{\mathbf{x}}_{s})\mbox{d}s</annotation></semantics></math>, called a flow.
This means that once <math id="S3.SS4.p2.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta</annotation></semantics></math> is obtained, data sampling can be performed by solving Eq. (<a href="#S3.E13" title="In III-D Flow Matching Approach ‣ III Proposed: LatentVoiceGrad ‣ LatentVoiceGrad: Nonparallel Voice Conversion with Latent Diffusion/Flow-Matching Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">13</span></a>) with <math id="S3.SS4.p2.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\mathbf{u}}_{t}(\bm{\mathbf{x}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐮</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\mathbf{u}}_{t}(\bm{\mathbf{x}})</annotation></semantics></math> replaced by <math id="S3.SS4.p2.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\mathbf{v}}_{\theta}(\bm{\mathbf{x}},t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐯</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\mathbf{v}}_{\theta}(\bm{\mathbf{x}},t)</annotation></semantics></math>, specifically by numerically computing <math id="S3.SS4.p2.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\mathbf{x}}_{1}=\bm{\mathbf{\phi}}_{1}(\bm{\mathbf{x}}_{0})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϕ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\mathbf{x}}_{1}=\bm{\mathbf{\phi}}_{1}(\bm{\mathbf{x}}_{0})</annotation></semantics></math>, using methods such as Euler’s method.
Therefore, our focus is on how to train <math id="S3.SS4.p2.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta</annotation></semantics></math> using the training examples of <math id="S3.SS4.p2.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\mathbf{x}}_{1}\sim q_{1}(\bm{\mathbf{x}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>∼</mo><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\mathbf{x}}_{1}\sim q_{1}(\bm{\mathbf{x}})</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS4.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS4.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Assuming <math id="S3.SS4.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\mathbf{x}}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\mathbf{x}}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> is a sample drawn from a simple distribution <math id="S3.SS4.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>q</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> (e.g., a standard normal distribution), the density <math id="S3.SS4.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S3.SS4.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\mathbf{x}}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\mathbf{x}}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> at time <math id="S3.SS4.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>, called a probability path, can be described using <math id="S3.SS4.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>q</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS4.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\mathbf{\phi}}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϕ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\mathbf{\phi}}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> via the change of variables formula (or push-forward equation).
To determine if a given vector field <math id="S3.SS4.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\mathbf{v}}_{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐯</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\mathbf{v}}_{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math> can generate a valid probability path <math id="S3.SS4.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>, one practical approach is to test if <math id="S3.SS4.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\mathbf{v}}_{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐯</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\mathbf{v}}_{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS4.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> satisfy the continuity equation</p>
<table id="S3.EGx11" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E14"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\frac{\mbox{d}p_{t}(\bm{\mathbf{x}})}{\mbox{d}t}+\nabla\cdot(p_{t}(\bm{\mathbf{x}})\bm{\mathbf{v}}_{\theta}(\bm{\mathbf{x}},t))=0." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mtext>d</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mtext>d</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo>+</mo><mrow><mo>∇</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐯</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\frac{\mbox{d}p_{t}(\bm{\mathbf{x}})}{\mbox{d}t}+\nabla\cdot(p_{t}(\bm{\mathbf{x}})\bm{\mathbf{v}}_{\theta}(\bm{\mathbf{x}},t))=0.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(14)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS4.p3.2" class="ltx_p">Under this constraint, the goal is to find <math id="S3.SS4.p3.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S3.SS4.p3.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{1}=q_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{1}=q_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>.
In CNF models, <math id="S3.SS4.p3.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta</annotation></semantics></math> is trained to directly maximize the log-likelihood <math id="S3.SS4.p3.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{E}_{\bm{\mathbf{x}}_{1}\sim q_{1}(\bm{\mathbf{x}})}[\log p_{1}(\bm{\mathbf{x}}_{1})]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>∼</mo><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{E}_{\bm{\mathbf{x}}_{1}\sim q_{1}(\bm{\mathbf{x}})}[\log p_{1}(\bm{\mathbf{x}}_{1})]</annotation></semantics></math>, which can be derived from Eq. (<a href="#S3.E14" title="In III-D Flow Matching Approach ‣ III Proposed: LatentVoiceGrad ‣ LatentVoiceGrad: Nonparallel Voice Conversion with Latent Diffusion/Flow-Matching Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">14</span></a>). However, this approach can be computationally very expensive because both evaluating and differentiating <math id="S3.SS4.p3.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\log p_{1}(\bm{\mathbf{x}}_{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\log p_{1}(\bm{\mathbf{x}}_{1})</annotation></semantics></math> require integration over time for each individual sample.
Instead of using the log-likelihood, if we know the real vector field <math id="S3.SS4.p3.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\mathbf{u}}_{t}(\bm{\mathbf{x}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐮</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\mathbf{u}}_{t}(\bm{\mathbf{x}})</annotation></semantics></math>, we might want to use a loss function that directly measures the distance between <math id="S3.SS4.p3.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\mathbf{v}}_{\theta}(\bm{\mathbf{x}},t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐯</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\mathbf{v}}_{\theta}(\bm{\mathbf{x}},t)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS4.p3.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\mathbf{u}}_{t}(\bm{\mathbf{x}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐮</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\mathbf{u}}_{t}(\bm{\mathbf{x}})</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="S3.EGx12" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E15"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathcal{L}_{\rm FM}(\theta)=\mathbb{E}_{t,\bm{\mathbf{x}}\sim p_{t}(\bm{\mathbf{x}})}[\|\bm{\mathbf{v}}_{\theta}(\bm{\mathbf{x}},t)-\bm{\mathbf{u}}_{t}(\bm{\mathbf{x}})\|_{2}^{2}]," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>FM</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐱</mi></mrow><mo>∼</mo><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝐯</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐮</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathcal{L}_{\rm FM}(\theta)=\mathbb{E}_{t,\bm{\mathbf{x}}\sim p_{t}(\bm{\mathbf{x}})}[\|\bm{\mathbf{v}}_{\theta}(\bm{\mathbf{x}},t)-\bm{\mathbf{u}}_{t}(\bm{\mathbf{x}})\|_{2}^{2}],</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(15)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS4.p3.3" class="ltx_p">as the training objective instead.
This loss is appealing because it avoids integration over <math id="S3.SS4.p3.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> and can be evaluated at each <math id="S3.SS4.p3.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> independently.
Unfortunately, this loss is intractable to use in practice because both <math id="S3.SS4.p3.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\mathbf{u}}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐮</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\mathbf{u}}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS4.p3.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> are not directly accesible.
However, an interesting fact is that if we express <math id="S3.SS4.p3.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{t}(\bm{\mathbf{x}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{t}(\bm{\mathbf{x}})</annotation></semantics></math> as a mixture of “conditional probability paths” <math id="S3.SS4.p3.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{t}(\bm{\mathbf{x}}|\bm{\mathbf{c}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>𝐜</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{t}(\bm{\mathbf{x}}|\bm{\mathbf{c}})</annotation></semantics></math> that vary according to some conditioning variable <math id="S3.SS4.p3.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\mathbf{c}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐜</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\mathbf{c}}</annotation></semantics></math></p>
<table id="S3.EGx13" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E16"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E16.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle p_{t}(\bm{\mathbf{x}})=\int p_{t}(\bm{\mathbf{x}}|\bm{\mathbf{c}})\pi(\bm{\mathbf{c}})\mbox{d}\bm{\mathbf{c}}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mrow><mo>∫</mo><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>𝐜</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐜</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>d</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐜</mi></mrow></mrow></mstyle></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle p_{t}(\bm{\mathbf{x}})=\int p_{t}(\bm{\mathbf{x}}|\bm{\mathbf{c}})\pi(\bm{\mathbf{c}})\mbox{d}\bm{\mathbf{c}},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(16)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS4.p3.4" class="ltx_p">a loss function defined as</p>
<table id="S3.E17" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E17.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\rm CFM}(\theta)\\
=\mathbb{E}_{t,\bm{\mathbf{c}}\sim\pi(\bm{\mathbf{c}}),\bm{\mathbf{x}}\sim p_{t}(\bm{\mathbf{x}}|\bm{\mathbf{c}})}[\|\bm{\mathbf{v}}_{\theta}(\bm{\mathbf{x}},t)-\bm{\mathbf{u}}_{t}(\bm{\mathbf{x}}|\bm{\mathbf{c}})\|_{2}^{2}]," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>CFM</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐜</mi></mrow><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐜</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>∼</mo><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>𝐜</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝐯</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐮</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>𝐜</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\rm CFM}(\theta)\\
=\mathbb{E}_{t,\bm{\mathbf{c}}\sim\pi(\bm{\mathbf{c}}),\bm{\mathbf{x}}\sim p_{t}(\bm{\mathbf{x}}|\bm{\mathbf{c}})}[\|\bm{\mathbf{v}}_{\theta}(\bm{\mathbf{x}},t)-\bm{\mathbf{u}}_{t}(\bm{\mathbf{x}}|\bm{\mathbf{c}})\|_{2}^{2}],</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(17)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS4.p3.5" class="ltx_p">where
<math id="S3.SS4.p3.m27" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi</annotation></semantics></math> represents the distribution over <math id="S3.SS4.p3.m28" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\mathbf{c}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐜</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\mathbf{c}}</annotation></semantics></math> and
<math id="S3.SS4.p3.m29" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\mathbf{u}}_{t}(\bm{\mathbf{x}}|\bm{\mathbf{c}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐮</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>𝐜</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\mathbf{u}}_{t}(\bm{\mathbf{x}}|\bm{\mathbf{c}})</annotation></semantics></math> represents the vector field associated with <math id="S3.SS4.p3.m30" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{t}(\bm{\mathbf{x}}|\bm{\mathbf{c}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>𝐜</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{t}(\bm{\mathbf{x}}|\bm{\mathbf{c}})</annotation></semantics></math>,
is equal to <math id="S3.SS4.p3.m31" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\rm FM}(\theta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>FM</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\rm FM}(\theta)</annotation></semantics></math> up to a constant independent of <math id="S3.SS4.p3.m32" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta</annotation></semantics></math>.
While this holds for arbitrary <math id="S3.SS4.p3.m33" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\mathbf{c}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐜</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\mathbf{c}}</annotation></semantics></math>,
one convenient assumption for <math id="S3.SS4.p3.m34" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{t}(\bm{\mathbf{x}}|\bm{\mathbf{c}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>𝐜</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{t}(\bm{\mathbf{x}}|\bm{\mathbf{c}})</annotation></semantics></math> is given by Tong et al. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib59" title="" class="ltx_ref">59</a>]</cite>, who propose putting <math id="S3.SS4.p3.m35" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\mathbf{c}}=(\bm{\mathbf{x}}_{0},\bm{\mathbf{x}}_{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐜</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\mathbf{c}}=(\bm{\mathbf{x}}_{0},\bm{\mathbf{x}}_{1})</annotation></semantics></math> and assuming <math id="S3.SS4.p3.m36" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{t}(\bm{\mathbf{x}}|\bm{\mathbf{x}}_{0},\bm{\mathbf{x}}_{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{t}(\bm{\mathbf{x}}|\bm{\mathbf{x}}_{0},\bm{\mathbf{x}}_{1})</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS4.p3.m37" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi(\bm{\mathbf{x}}_{0},\bm{\mathbf{x}}_{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi(\bm{\mathbf{x}}_{0},\bm{\mathbf{x}}_{1})</annotation></semantics></math> as</p>
<table id="S3.EGx14" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E18"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E18.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle p_{t}(\bm{\mathbf{x}}|\bm{\mathbf{x}}_{0},\bm{\mathbf{x}}_{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle p_{t}(\bm{\mathbf{x}}|\bm{\mathbf{x}}_{0},\bm{\mathbf{x}}_{1})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E18.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\mathcal{N}(\bm{\mathbf{x}}|t\bm{\mathbf{x}}_{1}+(1-t)\bm{\mathbf{x}}_{0},\sigma^{2}\bm{\mathbf{I}})," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐈</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\mathcal{N}(\bm{\mathbf{x}}|t\bm{\mathbf{x}}_{1}+(1-t)\bm{\mathbf{x}}_{0},\sigma^{2}\bm{\mathbf{I}}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(18)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S3.E19"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E19.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\pi(\bm{\mathbf{x}}_{0},\bm{\mathbf{x}}_{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\pi(\bm{\mathbf{x}}_{0},\bm{\mathbf{x}}_{1})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E19.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=q_{0}(\bm{\mathbf{x}}_{0})q_{1}(\bm{\mathbf{x}}_{1})," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=q_{0}(\bm{\mathbf{x}}_{0})q_{1}(\bm{\mathbf{x}}_{1}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(19)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS4.p3.6" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS4.p3.m38" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> is a manually set constant.
Under these settings,
it can be confirmed from Eq. (<a href="#S3.E16" title="In III-D Flow Matching Approach ‣ III Proposed: LatentVoiceGrad ‣ LatentVoiceGrad: Nonparallel Voice Conversion with Latent Diffusion/Flow-Matching Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">16</span></a>) that
<math id="S3.SS4.p3.m39" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{1}(\bm{\mathbf{x}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{1}(\bm{\mathbf{x}})</annotation></semantics></math>
becomes</p>
<table id="S3.EGx15" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E20"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E20.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle p_{1}(\bm{\mathbf{x}})=\int q_{1}(\bm{\mathbf{x}}_{1})\mathcal{N}(\bm{\mathbf{x}}|\bm{\mathbf{x}}_{1},\sigma^{2}\bm{\mathbf{I}})\mbox{d}\bm{\mathbf{x}}_{1}\approx q_{1}(\bm{\mathbf{x}})," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mrow><mo>∫</mo><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐈</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>d</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></mrow></mstyle><mo>≈</mo><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle p_{1}(\bm{\mathbf{x}})=\int q_{1}(\bm{\mathbf{x}}_{1})\mathcal{N}(\bm{\mathbf{x}}|\bm{\mathbf{x}}_{1},\sigma^{2}\bm{\mathbf{I}})\mbox{d}\bm{\mathbf{x}}_{1}\approx q_{1}(\bm{\mathbf{x}}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(20)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS4.p3.7" class="ltx_p">which is a Gaussian mixture with each Gaussian centered at an individual training example <math id="S3.SS4.p3.m40" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\mathbf{x}}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\mathbf{x}}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Eq. (<a href="#S3.E18" title="In III-D Flow Matching Approach ‣ III Proposed: LatentVoiceGrad ‣ LatentVoiceGrad: Nonparallel Voice Conversion with Latent Diffusion/Flow-Matching Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">18</span></a>)
implies that
<math id="S3.SS4.p3.m41" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{t}(\bm{\mathbf{x}}|\bm{\mathbf{x}}_{0},\bm{\mathbf{x}}_{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{t}(\bm{\mathbf{x}}|\bm{\mathbf{x}}_{0},\bm{\mathbf{x}}_{1})</annotation></semantics></math> is represented as a Gaussian with variance <math id="S3.SS4.p3.m42" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> whose center moves in a straight line from <math id="S3.SS4.p3.m43" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\mathbf{x}}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\mathbf{x}}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S3.SS4.p3.m44" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\mathbf{x}}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\mathbf{x}}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> at a constant velocity as <math id="S3.SS4.p3.m45" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> varies from 0 to 1.
This corresponds to assuming <math id="S3.SS4.p3.m46" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\mathbf{x}}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\mathbf{x}}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> as</p>
<table id="S3.EGx16" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E21"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E21.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\bm{\mathbf{x}}_{t}=t\bm{\mathbf{x}}_{1}+(1-t)\bm{\mathbf{x}}_{0}+\sigma\bm{\mathbf{\epsilon}}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϵ</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\bm{\mathbf{x}}_{t}=t\bm{\mathbf{x}}_{1}+(1-t)\bm{\mathbf{x}}_{0}+\sigma\bm{\mathbf{\epsilon}},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(21)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS4.p3.8" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS4.p3.m47" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\mathbf{\epsilon}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϵ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\mathbf{\epsilon}}</annotation></semantics></math> is a random variable that follows a standard Gaussian.
Hence, from Eq. (<a href="#S3.E13" title="In III-D Flow Matching Approach ‣ III Proposed: LatentVoiceGrad ‣ LatentVoiceGrad: Nonparallel Voice Conversion with Latent Diffusion/Flow-Matching Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">13</span></a>), the vector field <math id="S3.SS4.p3.m48" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\mathbf{u}}_{t}(\bm{\mathbf{x}}|\bm{\mathbf{x}}_{0},\bm{\mathbf{x}}_{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐮</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\mathbf{u}}_{t}(\bm{\mathbf{x}}|\bm{\mathbf{x}}_{0},\bm{\mathbf{x}}_{1})</annotation></semantics></math> associated with the assumed <math id="S3.SS4.p3.m49" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{t}(\bm{\mathbf{x}}|\bm{\mathbf{x}}_{0},\bm{\mathbf{x}}_{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{t}(\bm{\mathbf{x}}|\bm{\mathbf{x}}_{0},\bm{\mathbf{x}}_{1})</annotation></semantics></math> is given explicitly as</p>
<table id="S3.EGx17" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E22"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E22.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\bm{\mathbf{u}}_{t}(\bm{\mathbf{x}}|\bm{\mathbf{x}}_{0},\bm{\mathbf{x}}_{1})=\frac{\mbox{d}\bm{\mathbf{x}}_{t}}{\mbox{d}t}=\bm{\mathbf{x}}_{1}-\bm{\mathbf{x}}_{0}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝐮</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mtext>d</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mrow><mtext>d</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\bm{\mathbf{u}}_{t}(\bm{\mathbf{x}}|\bm{\mathbf{x}}_{0},\bm{\mathbf{x}}_{1})=\frac{\mbox{d}\bm{\mathbf{x}}_{t}}{\mbox{d}t}=\bm{\mathbf{x}}_{1}-\bm{\mathbf{x}}_{0}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(22)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS4.p3.9" class="ltx_p">Assuming <math id="S3.SS4.p3.m50" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q_{0}=\mathcal{N}(\bm{\mathbf{0}},\bm{\mathbf{I}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>𝟎</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐈</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q_{0}=\mathcal{N}(\bm{\mathbf{0}},\bm{\mathbf{I}})</annotation></semantics></math> and
plugging (<a href="#S3.E18" title="In III-D Flow Matching Approach ‣ III Proposed: LatentVoiceGrad ‣ LatentVoiceGrad: Nonparallel Voice Conversion with Latent Diffusion/Flow-Matching Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">18</span></a>), (<a href="#S3.E19" title="In III-D Flow Matching Approach ‣ III Proposed: LatentVoiceGrad ‣ LatentVoiceGrad: Nonparallel Voice Conversion with Latent Diffusion/Flow-Matching Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">19</span></a>), and (<a href="#S3.E22" title="In III-D Flow Matching Approach ‣ III Proposed: LatentVoiceGrad ‣ LatentVoiceGrad: Nonparallel Voice Conversion with Latent Diffusion/Flow-Matching Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">22</span></a>)
into Eq. (<a href="#S3.E17" title="In III-D Flow Matching Approach ‣ III Proposed: LatentVoiceGrad ‣ LatentVoiceGrad: Nonparallel Voice Conversion with Latent Diffusion/Flow-Matching Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">17</span></a>), <math id="S3.SS4.p3.m51" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\rm CFM}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>CFM</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\rm CFM}</annotation></semantics></math> can be written as</p>
<table id="S3.EGx18" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E23"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E23.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathcal{L}_{\rm CFM}(\theta)=\mathbb{E}[\|\bm{\mathbf{v}}_{\theta}(\bm{\mathbf{x}}_{t},t)-(\bm{\mathbf{x}}_{1}-\bm{\mathbf{x}}_{0})\|_{2}^{2}]," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>CFM</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝐯</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathcal{L}_{\rm CFM}(\theta)=\mathbb{E}[\|\bm{\mathbf{v}}_{\theta}(\bm{\mathbf{x}}_{t},t)-(\bm{\mathbf{x}}_{1}-\bm{\mathbf{x}}_{0})\|_{2}^{2}],</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(23)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS4.p3.10" class="ltx_p">where the expectation is taken over
<math id="S3.SS4.p3.m52" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\sim\mathcal{U}(0,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\sim\mathcal{U}(0,1)</annotation></semantics></math>,
<math id="S3.SS4.p3.m53" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\mathbf{x}}_{0}\sim\mathcal{N}(\bm{\mathbf{x}}|\bm{\mathbf{0}},\bm{\mathbf{I}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><mn>𝟎</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐈</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\mathbf{x}}_{0}\sim\mathcal{N}(\bm{\mathbf{x}}|\bm{\mathbf{0}},\bm{\mathbf{I}})</annotation></semantics></math>,
<math id="S3.SS4.p3.m54" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\mathbf{x}}_{1}\sim q_{1}(\bm{\mathbf{x}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>∼</mo><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\mathbf{x}}_{1}\sim q_{1}(\bm{\mathbf{x}})</annotation></semantics></math>, and
<math id="S3.SS4.p3.m55" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\mathbf{x}}_{t}\sim\mathcal{N}(\bm{\mathbf{x}}|t\bm{\mathbf{x}}_{1}+(1-t)\bm{\mathbf{x}}_{0},\sigma^{2}\bm{\mathbf{I}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐈</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\mathbf{x}}_{t}\sim\mathcal{N}(\bm{\mathbf{x}}|t\bm{\mathbf{x}}_{1}+(1-t)\bm{\mathbf{x}}_{0},\sigma^{2}\bm{\mathbf{I}})</annotation></semantics></math>,
with <math id="S3.SS4.p3.m56" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{U}(0,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{U}(0,1)</annotation></semantics></math> representing a continuous uniform distribution over the range <math id="S3.SS4.p3.m57" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(0,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(0,1)</annotation></semantics></math>.
Notably, unlike the objective function in Eq. (<a href="#S3.E15" title="In III-D Flow Matching Approach ‣ III Proposed: LatentVoiceGrad ‣ LatentVoiceGrad: Nonparallel Voice Conversion with Latent Diffusion/Flow-Matching Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">15</span></a>), this objective can be easily evaluated and differentiated with respect to <math id="S3.SS4.p3.m58" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS4.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS4.p4.1" class="ltx_p">In DPM, the score network is trained to predict the noise added at each time step. The negative of this noise indicates the direction in which the data samples should be updated during the reverse diffusion process.
DPM and FM are conceptually similar in this respect because the vector field <math id="S3.SS4.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\mathbf{v}}_{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐯</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\mathbf{v}}_{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math> represents the direction in which the data sample should be updated at each step of the ODE solving process.
The difference is that Eq. (<a href="#S3.E23" title="In III-D Flow Matching Approach ‣ III Proposed: LatentVoiceGrad ‣ LatentVoiceGrad: Nonparallel Voice Conversion with Latent Diffusion/Flow-Matching Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">23</span></a>) ensures the vector fields at all points in the neighborhood of the trajectory from the initial point <math id="S3.SS4.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\mathbf{x}}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\mathbf{x}}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> to the real data sample <math id="S3.SS4.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\mathbf{x}}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\mathbf{x}}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> are oriented in the direction of <math id="S3.SS4.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\mathbf{x}}_{1}-\bm{\mathbf{x}}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\mathbf{x}}_{1}-\bm{\mathbf{x}}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> during training. This makes the trajectories of the ODE straight, allowing the ODE solving process to be more efficient than the reverse diffusion process in DPM.
Given the similarity between the two concepts and the advantage of FM, we can consider another version of VoiceGrad that replaces DPM with FM. Namely, just as the score network conditioned on a target speaker embedding in VoiceGrad can convert the mel-spectrogram of source speech to the target speaker’s voice through the reverse diffusion process, it is expected that the vector field conditioned on the target speaker embedding can achieve the same more efficiently through the ODE solving process.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS4.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS4.p5.1" class="ltx_p">In the FM version of VoiceGrad, the vector field network <math id="S3.SS4.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\mathbf{v}}_{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐯</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\mathbf{v}}_{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math> is designed to be conditioned on the speaker embedding <math id="S3.SS4.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\mathbf{s}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐬</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\mathbf{s}}</annotation></semantics></math>, time <math id="S3.SS4.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>, and phoneme embedding sequence <math id="S3.SS4.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\mathbf{p}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐩</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\mathbf{p}}</annotation></semantics></math>.
It is then trained using speech samples from various speakers using the <math id="S3.SS4.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> measure instead of the <math id="S3.SS4.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> measure</p>
<table id="S3.EGx19" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E24"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E24.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathcal{L}_{\rm CFM}(\theta)=\mathbb{E}[\|\bm{\mathbf{v}}_{\theta}(\bm{\mathbf{x}}_{t},t,\bm{\mathbf{s}},\bm{\mathbf{p}})-(\bm{\mathbf{x}}_{1}-\bm{\mathbf{x}}_{0})\|_{1}]," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>CFM</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝐯</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐬</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐩</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathcal{L}_{\rm CFM}(\theta)=\mathbb{E}[\|\bm{\mathbf{v}}_{\theta}(\bm{\mathbf{x}}_{t},t,\bm{\mathbf{s}},\bm{\mathbf{p}})-(\bm{\mathbf{x}}_{1}-\bm{\mathbf{x}}_{0})\|_{1}],</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(24)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS4.p5.2" class="ltx_p">as the training objective, where the expectation is taken over
<math id="S3.SS4.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\sim\mathcal{U}(0,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\sim\mathcal{U}(0,1)</annotation></semantics></math>,
<math id="S3.SS4.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\sim\mathcal{U}[1,\ldots,K]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\sim\mathcal{U}[1,\ldots,K]</annotation></semantics></math>,
<math id="S3.SS4.p5.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\mathbf{x}}_{0}\sim\mathcal{N}(\bm{\mathbf{x}}|\bm{\mathbf{0}},\bm{\mathbf{I}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><mn>𝟎</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐈</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\mathbf{x}}_{0}\sim\mathcal{N}(\bm{\mathbf{x}}|\bm{\mathbf{0}},\bm{\mathbf{I}})</annotation></semantics></math>,
<math id="S3.SS4.p5.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\mathbf{x}}_{1}\sim q_{1}(\bm{\mathbf{x}}|k)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>∼</mo><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\mathbf{x}}_{1}\sim q_{1}(\bm{\mathbf{x}}|k)</annotation></semantics></math>, and
<math id="S3.SS4.p5.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\mathbf{x}}_{t}\sim\mathcal{N}(\bm{\mathbf{x}}|t\bm{\mathbf{x}}_{1}+(1-t)\bm{\mathbf{x}}_{0},\sigma^{2}\bm{\mathbf{I}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐈</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\mathbf{x}}_{t}\sim\mathcal{N}(\bm{\mathbf{x}}|t\bm{\mathbf{x}}_{1}+(1-t)\bm{\mathbf{x}}_{0},\sigma^{2}\bm{\mathbf{I}})</annotation></semantics></math>,
<math id="S3.SS4.p5.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\mathbf{x}}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\mathbf{x}}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> represents the mel-spectrogram of a training utterance,
<math id="S3.SS4.p5.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> represents a speaker index,
<math id="S3.SS4.p5.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q_{1}(\bm{\mathbf{x}}|k)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q_{1}(\bm{\mathbf{x}}|k)</annotation></semantics></math> represents the distribution of the mel-spectrograms of speaker <math id="S3.SS4.p5.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>,
<math id="S3.SS4.p5.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> is the number of speakers included in the training set,
<math id="S3.SS4.p5.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\mathbf{s}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐬</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\mathbf{s}}</annotation></semantics></math> represents the speaker embedding extracted from <math id="S3.SS4.p5.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\mathbf{x}}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\mathbf{x}}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>,
and <math id="S3.SS4.p5.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\mathbf{p}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐩</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\mathbf{p}}</annotation></semantics></math> represents the phoneme embedding sequence extracted from <math id="S3.SS4.p5.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\mathbf{x}}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\mathbf{x}}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS4.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS4.p6.1" class="ltx_p">The FM version can also follow a similar approach to LatentVoiceGrad by treating the autoencoder bottleneck as the data to be transformed, training the vector field network instead of the score network, and using an ODE solver instead of reverse diffusion to perform VC.
In this case, if we use <math id="S3.SS4.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\mathbf{z}}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\mathbf{z}}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> to denote the autoencoder bottleneck features extracted from the mel-spectrogram <math id="S3.SS4.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\mathbf{x}}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\mathbf{x}}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> of a training utterance, the training objective will be the same as in Eq. (<a href="#S3.E24" title="In III-D Flow Matching Approach ‣ III Proposed: LatentVoiceGrad ‣ LatentVoiceGrad: Nonparallel Voice Conversion with Latent Diffusion/Flow-Matching Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">24</span></a>), but with <math id="S3.SS4.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\mathbf{x}}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\mathbf{x}}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> replaced by <math id="S3.SS4.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\mathbf{z}}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\mathbf{z}}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>.
This results in four possible approaches, depending on whether we use the mel-spectrogram or the autoencoder bottleneck as the data to be converted, and whether the DPM or FM model serves as the underlying generative model.
We will refer to these as VoiceGrad-DPM, LatentVoiceGrad-DPM, VoiceGrad-FM, and LatentVoiceGrad-FM, and compare them experimentally in the following section.
The VC processes of VoiceGrad-FM and LatentVoiceGrad-FM using Euler’s method are outlined in Algorithms <a href="#alg3" title="Algorithm 3 ‣ III-D Flow Matching Approach ‣ III Proposed: LatentVoiceGrad ‣ LatentVoiceGrad: Nonparallel Voice Conversion with Latent Diffusion/Flow-Matching Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> and <a href="#alg4" title="Algorithm 4 ‣ III-D Flow Matching Approach ‣ III Proposed: LatentVoiceGrad ‣ LatentVoiceGrad: Nonparallel Voice Conversion with Latent Diffusion/Flow-Matching Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>, respectively.
Both algorithms were found to perform well when random noise was added to the initial point, using <math id="S3.SS4.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\mathbf{x}}\leftarrow(1-r)\bm{\mathbf{x}}+r\bm{\mathbf{\epsilon}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐱</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϵ</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\mathbf{x}}\leftarrow(1-r)\bm{\mathbf{x}}+r\bm{\mathbf{\epsilon}}</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="S3.SS4.p6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\mathbf{z}}\leftarrow(1-r)\bm{\mathbf{z}}+r\bm{\mathbf{\epsilon}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐳</mi><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐳</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϵ</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\mathbf{z}}\leftarrow(1-r)\bm{\mathbf{z}}+r\bm{\mathbf{\epsilon}}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S3.SS4.p6.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\mathbf{\epsilon}}\sim\mathcal{N}(\bm{\mathbf{0}},\bm{\mathbf{I}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϵ</mi><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>𝟎</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐈</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\mathbf{\epsilon}}\sim\mathcal{N}(\bm{\mathbf{0}},\bm{\mathbf{I}})</annotation></semantics></math> represents Gaussian noise and <math id="S3.SS4.p6.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0\leq r\leq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0\leq r\leq 1</annotation></semantics></math> represents the proportion of noise added.</p>
</div>
<figure id="alg3" class="ltx_float ltx_float_algorithm ltx_framed ltx_framed_top">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_float"><span id="alg3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Algorithm 3</span> </span> VoiceGrad (FM version) VC process</figcaption>
