</figure>
<div id="S4.SS7.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS7.p3.1" class="ltx_p">In general, with Euler’s method, increasing the number of iterations improves the accuracy of the ODE solution. Therefore, it is crucial to understand how the quality of the converted speech varies with different settings of <math id="S4.SS7.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math>. The results in Table <a href="#S4.T7" title="TABLE VII ‣ IV-G Impact of r and L Settings in FM Versions ‣ IV Experiments ‣ LatentVoiceGrad: Nonparallel Voice Conversion with Latent Diffusion/Flow-Matching Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">VII</span></a> show that, as expected, increasing <math id="S4.SS7.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> improves performance. However, it is noteworthy that even with just 3 iterations, the performance remains relatively high. This suggests that the FM version can deliver good results with a small number of iterations, which helps reduce processing time.
Since the SECS increases steadily with higher <math id="S4.SS7.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> values, it may be beneficial to use a moderate value like 10 in scenarios where speaker similarity is particularly important. The CER results also confirm that the intelligibility of the converted speech is well maintained when <math id="S4.SS7.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> is greater than 2.</p>
</div>
